(A) $\mathrm{ICl}_4^{-}:$ Steric Number =$\frac{1}{8}$(7+(4 \times 7)+1)=$\frac{36}{8}=$4+4$; b.p. $=4$, e.p. =\frac{4}{2}=2\therefore \quad \mathrm{ICl}_4^{-} \rightarrow \mathrm{sp}^3 \mathrm{ d}^2  hybridization
(B)

 \begin{aligned}& \mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}: \frac{1}{8}(5+(3 \times6)+1) \\
& =\frac{1}{8} \times24=3 \text{ b.p., } \mathrm{sp}^2 \text{ hybridization }
\end{aligned} 

(C)

 \begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{PCl}_{4}^{+}: \frac{1}{8}(5+(4 \times7)-1) \\
& =\frac{1}{8} \times32=4 \text{ b.p., } \mathrm{sp}^3 \text{ hybridization }
\end{aligned} 

(D)

 \begin{aligned}& \mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}: \frac{1}{8}(4+(6 \times7)+2) \\
& =\frac{1}{8} \times48=6 \text{ b.p., } \mathrm{sp}^3 \mathrm{ d}^2 \text{ hybridization }
\end{aligned}$

questions.q:
How many oxides among the following are basic in nature?

$\begin{aligned}& \mathrm{As}_{2}  \mathrm{O}_{3}, \mathrm{Na}_{2}  \mathrm{O}, \mathrm{MgO}, \mathrm{Mn}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{3},  \mathrm{CaO}, \mathrm{ZnO}, \mathrm{N}_{2} \mathrm{O} \text{, }\\
& \mathrm{N}_{2}  \mathrm{O}_{5}
\end{aligned}
$

questions.solution:
Greater the bond order, shorter is the bond length and thus, stronger is the bond.

Since, for the given molecules, bond order follows the trend
$\begin{array}{ccc}\mathrm{N}_2 &  \gt \mathrm{O}_2 &  \gt \mathrm{F}_2 \\ (\mathrm{~B} . \mathrm{O}=3) & (\mathrm{B} . \mathrm{O}=2) & (\mathrm{B} . \mathrm{O}=1)\end{array}
Thus, the bond length decreases as \mathrm{F}}_{2}>\mathrm{O}$}_{2}>$\mathrm{N}}_{2}$ and bond dissociation energy follows the trend  $\mathrm{N}$}_{2}>$\mathrm{O}}_{2}> \mathrm{F}$}_{2}

(a) Hybridised orbitals show only head on overlapping and thus form only $\sigma$ bonds. They never form $\pi$ bonds.
(c) Head on overlapping is stronger than lateral or sideways overlapping. Therefore, the strength of bonds follows the order

\underbrace{\pi_{\ rho-p}}_{\begin{ array}{c}
$\text{ lateral }$\\
$\text{ overlapping }
\end{array}$} < \underbrace{\sigma_{s-s} < \sigma_{s-p} < \sigma_{\rho-p}}_{$\text{head on overlapping of same shell }$}

(d) s-orbitals are spherically symmetrical and thus show only head on overlapping and form only $\sigma$ bonds.
